<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hippocrates of Chios</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 470–c. 410 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippocrates">Hippocrates</a> of Chios was a Greek mathematician who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in the fifth century BCE. He wrote the first known systematic Elements of geometry, a forerunner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euclid">Euclid</a>'s, and is famous for showing that certain figures bounded by circular arcs, the lunes, have areas equal to rectilinear figures. He also reduced the problem of doubling the cube to finding two mean proportionals.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hippocrates-of-chios.jpg" alt="Hippocrates of Chios" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Audriusa assumed (based on copyright claims). · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hipocrat_arcs.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
